# Data Classification

This repository is public. Git history is permanent. Deleting a file does not remove it, and making the repo private later does not retract clones, forks, or caches.

Full rules: `docs/DATA_CLASSIFICATION.md`.

## The rule

Formulas are public. Numbers derived from real campaigns are not.

The arithmetic is commodity — majority is half plus one, attempts are universe times contact depth. Publishing it proves the product's central claim, that Wings is deterministic and auditable rather than an opaque model. What is proprietary is the empirical layer: what turnout actually runs at, what contact rates actually hold, what field work actually costs.

## The failure mode specific to AI-assisted work

An assistant asked for a "realistic" fixture will generate plausible-looking empirical values. A 62.4% high-frequency turnout rate, a 14.3% door contact rate, a $73.50 per-shift cost. These are indistinguishable from real calibration data to anyone reading the repo, and the automated guard cannot tell them apart either.

Do not do this. When a fixture needs a number, invent an obviously synthetic one.

Use round, checkable values: turnout of 0.5, a universe of 10,000, 40 attempts per shift, $100 per shift. A fixture exists to prove a formula is correct, not to demonstrate that a value is realistic. Realism is calibration's job and calibration is private.

If a scenario genuinely needs an empirical-looking value, say so and ask rather than inventing one.

## Never commit

- Calibration values: turnout probabilities, contact rates, flake rates, attempts per shift, unit costs, alert thresholds
- Anything from a source carrying a reuse restriction
- Client or campaign data, including aggregated and anonymized forms, and including test fixtures — the CD24 turnout geography is client data
- Campaign, candidate, district, staff, or vendor names
- Credentials, `.env` files, keys
- `.csv`, `.xlsx`, `.parquet`, or other bulk data files

## Check

`npm run check:classification` runs the guard. It catches credential shapes, voter-identifier field names, bulk data files, and restricted-source markers. It cannot judge whether a turnout rate came from a real campaign. That judgment is yours.
